WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.900
Fasting blood holds about one insulin molecule for every trillion water molecules.

2
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.800
How do you detect a signal that scarce, or locate one phosphate inside a cell?

3
00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.780
Albumin outnumbers insulin 10 million to 1.

4
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.000
Inside cells, thousands of proteins crowd together.

5
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:26.503
Start with a blood hormone, and let it compete for an antibody.

6
00:00:27.843 --> 00:00:32.663
Put the same small amount of antibody and radioactive hormone in every tube.

7
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:38.803
With few antibody sites, unlabelled sample hormone fights labelled hormone for

8
00:00:38.803 --> 00:00:45.123
space. Surprisingly, more sample hormone leaves less radioactivity on the antibody.

9
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:49.583
Here is the math when high-affinity sites stay nearly full.

10
00:00:51.043 --> 00:00:54.803
Labelled fraction equals tracer divided by tracer plus sample.

11
00:00:56.083 --> 00:01:01.163
Put 100 pM of tracer in every tube, giving 4,000 bound counts.

12
00:01:02.383 --> 00:01:04.423
Add 100 pM of sample.

13
00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:09.563
100 / 200 is 1/2, about 2,000 counts.

14
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.147
Test a richer sample.

15
00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:20.227
Suppose the sample adds 300 pM of unlabelled hormone to the 100 pM tracer.

16
00:01:21.627 --> 00:01:26.347
Tracer over tracer plus sample: what fraction stays labelled, and how many counts

17
00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:27.347
stay bound?

18
00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:40.630
100 / 400 is 1/4, so about 1/4 of 4,000, 1,000 counts, stays bound.

19
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.870
Known standards build a falling curve that reads unknowns at picomolar levels.

20
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.690
That is the radioimmunoassay.

21
00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.950
Its blind spot: the count is only as selective as the antibody.

22
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.690
Move inside the cell.

23
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:04.870
Heating a cell extract in the detergent SDS unfolds proteins and coats them with

24
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.170
negative charge in proportion to length.

25
00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:12.850
An electric field pulls them through a gel, and smaller proteins travel farther.

26
00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.010
Transfer the bands to a membrane for a western blot.

27
00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.650
A phosphospecific antibody, which binds only when one site carries phosphate, lights

28
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.650
up that band.

29
00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:28.783
Always blot the same samples for total protein too.

30
00:02:30.023 --> 00:02:34.683
Otherwise a darker band cannot separate more phosphate per molecule from more

31
00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:41.803
protein. An antibody also has a blind spot: raised against a known site, it cannot

32
00:02:41.803 --> 00:02:43.243
find an unsuspected one.

33
00:02:44.217 --> 00:02:47.337
To find an unknown site, weigh the pieces instead.

34
00:02:48.617 --> 00:02:53.257
Cut the protein into peptides with trypsin, and weigh each in a mass spectrometer.

35
00:02:54.557 --> 00:02:56.757
One phosphate adds about 80 Da.

36
00:02:58.117 --> 00:03:03.337
Tandem mass spectrometry adds a second round: break that heavier peptide along its

37
00:03:03.337 --> 00:03:04.937
backbone and weigh the fragments.

38
00:03:06.357 --> 00:03:10.197
Fragments carrying the extra 80 Da place it on one residue.

39
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:16.890
Mass spectrometry has a blind spot too: if no break falls between two candidate

40
00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.250
residues, the site stays ambiguous.

41
00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:25.830
Competition, antibodies, and mass spectrometry detect scarce signals, each with

42
00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:26.570
blind spots.
